1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,760
Morgan comes to Harbor's Friday planning table with three open questions.

2
00:00:04,220 --> 00:00:05,420
Is the room note still current?

3
00:00:05,860 --> 00:00:07,240
Who is checking the facilitator?

4
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:09,160
Did anyone approve the print request?

5
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:13,800
If the answers live in three people's heads, Morgan becomes the operating system.

6
00:00:14,260 --> 00:00:20,220
We already have receipts, a handoff, a rubric, a review posture, and two held decisions.

7
00:00:20,860 --> 00:00:25,240
Put them where a second team member could see the state and take the next authorized step.

8
00:00:26,080 --> 00:00:28,240
Chapter Nineteen calls this a cockpit.

9
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:44,700
Start with exceptions, not a wall of charts: the Session Option Brief is draft; the facilitator note is unconfirmed; the staffing hold names Morgan; the print request is unapproved; the next action is to request a current, permitted staffing note.

10
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:50,100
Add owner, age, cost signal, approval queue, and a link to the source trace.

11
00:00:50,600 --> 00:00:57,600
A teammate should be able to distinguish work that is waiting from work that has failed, and a failed check should offer a recovery path.

12
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:01,120
This is a paper sketch, not a screenshot of a running system.

13
00:01:01,660 --> 00:01:09,720
Use the chapter's four-part morning loop: scan the exceptions, sort by owner, signal the useful change, then step in where judgment is needed.

14
00:01:10,220 --> 00:01:20,840
For Harbor, Morgan scans the staffing hold, routes the request for a fresh note to the course coordinator, records that the print envelope is still closed, and leaves the session proposal on hold.

15
00:01:21,360 --> 00:01:26,480
There is no need to display individual speed rankings or make a teammate's latency look like failure.

16
00:01:26,480 --> 00:01:30,920
The cockpit should reduce guessing, not pressure people into faster approval.

17
00:01:31,520 --> 00:01:36,980
Chapter Twenty gives us an Unblock Ladder: auto-run, notify, ask, escalate.

18
00:01:37,460 --> 00:01:41,380
On a real system, each rung would need a defined authority window and trace.

19
00:01:41,820 --> 00:01:46,060
On Harbor's paper design, a status update may be drafted as a notify card.

20
00:01:46,560 --> 00:01:49,100
The staffing gap is an ask to Morgan for direction.

21
00:01:49,560 --> 00:01:54,120
A proposed announcement or purchase escalates to a separate, named human gate.

22
00:01:54,120 --> 00:01:57,880
A mobile card may point Morgan to the full brief and source status.

23
00:01:58,600 --> 00:02:01,440
It must never hide a missing note behind an approve button.

24
00:02:02,020 --> 00:02:04,260
The phone is a narrow doorway into the cockpit.

25
00:02:04,780 --> 00:02:10,180
A good card says what is held, why, who owns it, and where to open the complete evidence.

26
00:02:10,840 --> 00:02:14,600
It can invite a comment or route the issue within a previously defined scope.

27
00:02:15,040 --> 00:02:22,500
It cannot invent a new scope, promote memory, order printing, book a room, publish a session, or override a source failure.

28
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:25,540
Every paper disposition returns to the same trace.

29
00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:28,960
A small screen does not make a consequential decision smaller.

30
00:02:29,380 --> 00:02:29,700
Pause.

31
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:35,320
If Morgan handed this sketch to a colleague tomorrow morning, what would that colleague still have to ask?

32
00:02:36,120 --> 00:02:41,860
Sketch one Mission Control Cockpit card for Harbor and one mobile unblock message for the same held brief.

33
00:02:42,480 --> 00:02:49,420
Show status, blocker, owner, age, source pointer, next safe step, and decision destination.

34
00:02:49,420 --> 00:02:52,540
Assign each message to a ladder rung.

35
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:56,300
Label what can be handled in the sketch and what must open the full review.

36
00:02:56,760 --> 00:02:59,700
Include a route for stale evidence and a closed spend gate.

37
00:03:00,100 --> 00:03:02,320
Use a fictional or approved example.

38
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:09,120
Do not use private operational data or paste a real message, agent run, person, or client record.

39
00:03:54,840 --> 00:03:56,400
Save revision eleven as a draft.

40
00:03:56,840 --> 00:04:01,620
The test is simple: can a second person understand why the work is held and who can move it?

41
00:04:01,620 --> 00:04:08,460
Next we will show proof of that work safely, and test a new idea without pretending a score is permission to build.
